A||||29||DavidHenshaw||Fiero love problem||||1||
Z||000000||DavidHenshaw||01-26-2001||01:03 PM||||Does anyone else have this problem. <P>I really like my fiero, actually I love my fiero. The problem is my wife thinks I'm crazy or nuts and my daughter thinks I'm having a mid life crisis. I've owned many cars but never anything as pure fun as this is to drive. Also, IMHO the car has beautiful styling. <P>I've only had the car since last summer and need some advice. Should I internalize and not talk about how great the car is or should I keep talking and just get used to the strange looks, comments, etc.<P>Dave <P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||GeeDee86GT||01-26-2001||01:17 PM||||Welcome to the club, Dave.  Mine feel the same way, except my 17 year old son. I suspect he may like the Fiero for a different reason as he just fried my clutch. I finally decided to get one, and inside a week had 4.  Gosh, maybe it is a mid-life thing.  But they sure are fun...<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/garby_d/geedee.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||2xsess||01-26-2001||01:54 PM||||Is 25 mid-life? Actually I've loved them since I got my first one at 17. I internalize as far as my wife and mother go but I let loose when talking to my dad. I guess maybe it's some genetic thing that he passed down ot me because he's just as obsessed with Harleys.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG">   <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html</A> ||||reg||5
Z||000003||noyb||01-26-2001||03:25 PM||||I wouldn't worry too much about what the family thinks.  I bought my last summer for a restoration project.  Didn't do much to it, as I was having problems getting the title cleared and didn't want to waste any money.  But it sat in my garage all summer and I would talk about it until the wee hours.  Put it in storage for the winter and the title did finally clear.  Now, all I do is wish that it was spring so that I can bring it out of storage and begin my true love affair with it.  My wife introduced it to our friends and "my mistress".  Absolutely!||||reg||7
Z||000004||Old Lar||01-26-2001||03:51 PM||||My first Fiero was a mid life crisis car, but by then I was single and didn't need to explain to a wife.    It must be a guy thing. The car is fun to drive, looks nice and did I say fun to drive.<P>Maybe the wives would rather have you out boozing, chasing other women, gambling or tied up with sports.  Rather than have you out in the garage tinkering on you car.<P>"Men are from Mars Women are from Venus"||||reg||3
Z||000005||Got2manyfieros||01-26-2001||03:59 PM||||As for my .02 cents...I just keep talking about how great my car is.  When someone doesn't believe me, I take them through a couple of tight turns with it.  Soon enough, my dad had one, and a couple of my good friends are also cruising in fieros too.  If only the girlfreind appreciated them...||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||01-26-2001||04:08 PM||||As far as the family goes, not too much of a problem as it was my dad who got me into Fieros. My wife is pretty indulgent about my car, as long as I don't go too crazy with it. She enjoys riding around in it, likes it when I take offramps at 70 mph, NOT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> But a nice spring day with the roof off is good times.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000007||mrfixit58||01-26-2001||05:05 PM||||Mid life crisis? Maybe. But who cares!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> I love my little car. I'm 42 and I have personally owned a 72 Cuda, 69 Firebird coupe (auto), 69 Firebird (4-speed), and a 69 Chevelle SS 396. I currently own a 70 Monte Carlo SS 454, 68 RS Camaro coupe, a 69 Firebird 400 convertible, and a 65 Nove SS. But, I get the biggest kick out of driving my 87 GT 5-speed! I'm looking forward to building a custom Fiero with lots of mods including a 4.9 Caddy motor.<P>I'd buy more if I had more money, time, and storage!<P>Cruise ON!<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric_PA||01-26-2001||05:20 PM||||The only thing I have got to say about this is, I'm glad I'm not alone. I thought I was developing a problem, but evidently not.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I just can't wait till Spring.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000009||jimig||01-26-2001||05:36 PM||||Well, my wife tells me all the time that I love my cars more than her  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  But I have had my Fiero since it was brand new and that was 2 years before I met her.  Before we got married, I told her to never ask me to sell my cars (I had the Firebird at that time also).<P>My 2 older boys (7 and 5 years old) love the Fiero; and they can't even see out of the window when they sit in the seat!  But my wife doesn't want to hear about the cars.  This is partly due to the fact that we had to sell her car last year.  In 1998 I let her pick out a new car for the family.  It could be whatever she wanted.  She picked out a 1998 Chevrolet Monte Carlo Z34.  It was black with a graphite leather interior and every option available except a cell phone.  She loved that car, and would love to race people with it.  But last year we were expecting child number 4 and we sold the car to her cousin.  We then bought a new 2000 GMC Yukon SLE.  I love the truck, she only likes it, but it can haul the whole family.  Unfortunately, we lost that baby, but now she is expecting again, so we still need the truck.<P>Anyway, because of this, I did not get the Fiero out of storage till last October for a couple of months.<P>People think that is crazy for me to own a 2 seater car when I have kids.  But I don't car.  I cannot bear to part with my Fiero.  Besides, my wife and I actually have a lot of shared memories with the car.  We used it all the time before the kids came along.  We even took it on our honeymoon.  I remember on the way home going through Nebraska.  My wife doesn't like to drive stick, which my Fiero is, and is nervous about smashing the car up. Well, I told that I would help her shift to get on the highway.  We got going and I proceded to fall asleep.  I woke up a little while later and we were in the left lane doing 85 mph  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  She tried to tell me that she thought that she needed to downshift in order to slow down  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>In conclusion, just enjoy the car and if your wife doesn't want to talk about it, we are all here for you.<P>------------------<BR>jimig<BR>1985 Fiero GT<BR>1968 Firebird 400 convertible<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroJoe||01-26-2001||06:01 PM||||It's ok to love your fiero!  My friends really think it is a cool car, but none of them really care (or understand what I am saying) to hear me talk about it too much.  It gives me something to focus my attention on though, which is good.  Girls love it too, always a plus!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Ever since that faithful night that my dad send me out to the barn to get something and I found a little blue two seater with the keys in it, I have not been the same.<P>Fieroholics, rock on!<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||HAGS||01-26-2001||06:04 PM||||In all my conversations my Fiero comes up sooner or later, people tell me I need help, or should be checked out by a shrink..."did you hit your head?"...when I get one that runs like its supposed to then I'll just give them all test drives and re-school america!  My car was awesome when everything was working at the same time...If that wasnt peak performance then I really cant wait to see what a good SE or GT can do.  <BR>I can see myself owning atleast one Fiero for the rest of my life, im 18.||||reg||1
Z||000012||TheHans||01-26-2001||06:06 PM||||If it weren't for Fieros I wouldn't know what to talk about with my dad. If there's one thing we have in common more than anything its the love of the Fiero.<P> Almost brings a tear to my eye just writing about them on the forum <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||HAGS||01-26-2001||06:52 PM||||Yeah, my dad used to hate Fieros and at the last family meeting he defended it while we were all standing around the knocking duke.||||reg||1
Z||000014||v8vinnie||01-26-2001||10:36 PM||||yea me too, i have had 3 corvettes 1981, 1997, and now my 1967 coupe is for sale, when i seen tthis 1987gt v8, t tops, 5 speed i had to have it, it still needs more syspension tuning but im having a blast with it, im 43 and i love my american fiero , and love my yamaha vstar 1100, i wish i could afford to keep my 67 vette but i think its better off to some one who only wants a play toy, the hell with resale value, i live for today and plan for tommorow, we are here for a short time the cars will out live us, so im having fun, what do you think?||||reg||1
Z||000015||ToddAsh||01-26-2001||11:30 PM||||God I'm so glad to hear this. I'm in love with my Fiero. Have a '56 vette and a Big Block 4-speed '56 Chevy and I'll probably sell the Chevy to make room for an '88 GT.Bought my 14,000 mile '85 V6 SE in November and still can't stop smiling everytime I drive it.I'm 47,grew up(barely)in my father's gas station and have owned or had access to almost every kind of car because of my car fanatic father and uncles.Don't know how I missed these. Todd||||reg||1
Z||000016||ur2biteme||01-27-2001||01:18 AM||||guess i went through mid life crisis at 20 then.  i've had mine for 2 years, and became this enthusiastic about it from the start.  i'm finally getting to the point of getting the body fixed now.  well, gotta wait till monday to get it back from the shop though.  oh well. <P>------------------<BR>Bob's '87 econo coupe<BR>170k awaiting a tranny:(<BR> <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/ur2biteme" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/ur2biteme</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||CozmoPooky||01-27-2001||08:02 AM||||My g/f calls our club meetings "support groups", you know,<P>"Hi, my name is Cozmo, and I own Fieros".<P>I've had dozens of very cool and weird cars in my 46 years but Fieros are unique. I don't buy them, I "adopt" them...... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Gotta love it....<P>------------------<BR>CozmoPooky<BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>'87 Duke Coupe (4.9 Caddy conversion in progress...)<BR>'85 2M6 Targa||||reg||10
Z||000018||v8vinnie||01-27-2001||09:01 AM||||boy you should of heard my wife when i told her i was looking for a fiero, she gave me the same dumb look everyone else does,but that doesent bother me at all,i guess i like being the under dog ,buy less get more?. a lawyer friend in the gym i go to has a new 135,000 bmw zz8, he likes to be different ,he can afford a car like that i like toasting cars like that with my 11,000 fiero, i had a new 1997 c5 corvette and i got board of it in a year,( also the payments), i  am not going thru a mide life crissis i have always been i free bird, i love to come and go as i please, these young guys are going to get caught up with that family thing sooner or later and when they realize they,given up 15 to 20 years of their life, they will want it back, i never had children of my own, doesent make me a bad person , i just wanted it that way made that decision at 13, my wife has 2 kids now 17, 18 cant wait to they move ouy so i can get my life back and travel, so dont think or let people make fun of you guys, hell like one commedion said , once you marry them the average women gains 3 to 7 pounds a year, boy was he right , but will let the young guys find that on their own at least my car stays the same , just the way it was WHEN I FIRST MET HER!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000019||jerpro||01-27-2001||09:17 AM||||I had to make a pact with my fiancé. I get to keep both my fieros and she gets two cats and a truck. <BR>I'm ok with it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>The only thing that annoys me is when classmates in automotive class laugh at me for having a fiero. I get angry, but once I am inside my car and hit the first turn I am happy again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||KnightRyder31||01-27-2001||09:27 AM||||I love my Fiero...but everyone thinks that I'm crazy.<BR>I don't care what they think...I'll blow out any of thier rice burners any day!<P>||||reg||6
Z||000021||Greg Piet||01-27-2001||02:00 PM||||You ever hear the expression "You go before the dog?"<P>Well...  nuff said!<P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72<BR>#47 CSP<BR>Bring it to the track.<BR>Lets see if all those mods really helped!<p>[This message has been edited by Greg Piet (edited 01-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||fierobear||01-27-2001||03:02 PM||||I bought my first Fiero on a lark. I was looking for a cheap car to get around town in when I wasn't working, and saw it at a charity lot. I fell in love while driving it home, like I was hit by a lighting bolt.<P>I now own 6, some of which are parts and project cars. I have a definate car sickness and I need help.<P>I'm thinking of checking myself into the Henry Ford Clinic.||||reg||1
Z||000023||v8vinnie||01-27-2001||03:11 PM||||to day it seems like a new tribe of sub compact sports cars, may be thats one of the reasons i bought my fiero, it really has timeless looks and mordern style somewhat, i get a kick out of the wings i see on some of these rice burners, now i dont want to be a hippocrett, i do ride a jap bike , i have no choise, the make much better bikes than the other v twinn(harley) for a lot less price,and i dont care about resale value, been there don that, but i love my american cars so ill always stick with them,i cant wait untill my poly trans mounts come in and my bumpsteer kit gets here, new tires and im ready to toast every 4 foot wing high rice burner that is stupid enough to take on my 345hp v8 fiero, and that is called MIDLIFE CRISSES???? YEA BABY!||||reg||1
Z||000024||v8vinnie||01-27-2001||03:13 PM||||SORRY FOR THE MISSPELLING OF SOME WORDS , BUT THAT HAPPENES WHEN I GET EXCITED!!||||reg||1
Z||000025||Tina||01-27-2001||05:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DavidHenshaw:<BR><B>Does anyone else have this problem. <BR>I really like my fiero, actually I love my fiero.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not only addicting, but it is also contagious.<BR>Even boyfriend,who usually just  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>when I say anything remotely Fiero related.<BR>Last week asked me, if he should get one for <BR>himself.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tina.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||Motorhead||01-27-2001||08:03 PM||||When I bought my new one in '84 my wife and I were living together.  She cried the day I traded it for a '76 TR6 and an '82 Fiat Spyder. I was pretty un-enlightened back then!  The Fiat was her daily driver with my infant son riding shotgun and the Triumph was mine.  Suffice it to say we now have an '84 and '86 coupe sitting in the driveway.  Next to the '83 Datsun 280 ZX and of coarse the "newer" Geo Prizm!  It'd probably be a Honda if I had the money!<BR>Whoops, sorry! There is a Honda at the house!  A '72 CB750 "in the works".  Does that count?  <BR> <BR> <P>------------------<BR>"The one who dies with the most toys wins!"<BR>  bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1372350&a=10234998&p=38686771&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000027||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||01-27-2001||10:04 PM||||The love for Fieros was a re-awakening for me.  I drooled over Popular Mechanics and Road & Track articles about the Fieros when they appeared in 1983-1984.  I could  not think of any car.  The problem was that I was 14.  By the time I recieved my liscence, Beer was my only interest.  I drove a 50,000mile sunshine yellow 1978 Buick Skyhawk 3.8L V6 4spd. without catalytic converter which could smoke the tires in the first two gears.  I forgot about the Fiero.<BR>Now, I have two 1984s (parts car, driver) and I am once more completely obsessed. All of the wrenching has restored muscle tone like not fitness plan I have tried.  My wife thinks the car is cool and she is happy to see me so happy and healthy.  The car challenges my mind every time I work on it.  It challenges my body.  And, sometimes I drool when I look at it.  Okay, I drool as I dream about how incredible it will look when it is done.  The truth is however, that I am glad it is not.  The effort to restore the car is the part I find most addictive.<P>I should admit that a 1978 Buick Skyhawk in susnshine yellow would still get my attention.  I had more fun in the one I drove in high school than I did in any other car.  It had torque, decent handling, incredible gas mileage, and the most comfortable seats for my 6' body.  The only problem was a tail happy live axle rear suspension and completely ineffective brakes.  You could fishtail on any corner at any speed in any condition.  I thougt I was some kind of rally car driver everywhere I went.  Crude 70s crap car and yet marvelous.<BR>The Fiero reminds me of my old Buick in many ways, but handles so much better.  I have a new motorcycle and a Ford Ranger truck, but I only want to drive my Fiero now.  <BR>Is it an illness or just ignorance in those who do not share our love for these cars?  To date, nobody wants to get into my Fiero for a ride.  They just don't understand why they should ride in a "FEE-Yehr-Oh" as they say it.  Then when we go for a ride, they don't want to get out of the car.  It's like everybody has completely the wrong idea about these cars.  In return for putting up with their ignorance, we get the cheap!<BR>Cool is not about conformity or convenience.  In this way, Fieros are the coolest cars ever . . .||||reg||1
Z||000028||Cheever2||01-28-2001||12:10 AM||||My wife wouldn't be so bothered if I could spend some bucks on things we need like furniture instead of constantly spending on Fiero repairs.  She says "Fiero is the F-word to me".  Otherwise I think she was impressed with the maturity of the people at our first club meeting a few weeks ago.  <BR><P>------------------<BR><I>Chuck Hodges</I>||||reg||6
Z||000029||frontal lobe||01-28-2001||12:29 AM||||To answer your question, yes, you should just internalize your feelings for the Fiero around your wife.  If you feel a need to talk about how great it is, it's better to just do it here.  Some people are just never going to "get it", so just forget trying.<P>I'm 45 so I guess it was a legitimate question if I was having a mid-life crisis.  Answer is no, it just happened to me "mid-life" when I got financially to the point where I could spend some money on an extra car.  Even my insurance agent made a crack about mid-life crisis when I called him to get it insured.<P>My wife thought I should get my head examined for getting one so that's why I picked "frontal lobe" for a name--the part of the brain involved in "intelligent" thought.||||reg||1
